Pramipexole is marketed in two oral formulations: immediate-release tablets (Mirapexin; 0.125, 0.25, 0.5 mg, plus 1.0 mg for the Parkinson's schedule — dosed once nightly for RLS — as marketed, the EU RLS schedule runs 0.125 → 0.25 → 0.50 → 0.75 mg with a maximum of 0.75 mg/day (a product-marketing observation; no label source is cited here), above the paper's 0.5 mg/day working ceiling (@pwarn:pramipexole-mecfs-safety) — or three times daily for Parkinson's disease) and an extended-release formulation (Sifrol or Mirapexin PR in the EU, Mirapex ER in the US; 0.375, 0.75, 1.5, 4.5 mg — once daily). Three Phase I steady-state studies in healthy male volunteers established that the ER 0.75 mg once-daily dose approximates IR 0.25 mg three times daily: AUC(ss) 17.4 vs 16.0 ng·h/mL, Cmax,ss 0.967 vs 1.09 ng/mL, Cmin,ss 0.455 vs 0.383 ng/mL @Jenner2009PramipexoleERpk.

#clinical-finding(title: [Pramipexole IR and ER Are Steady-State Bioequivalent; ER Flattens the Peak])[
Jenner et al. (2009) compared once-daily extended-release pramipexole with immediate-release pramipexole dosed three times daily across three Phase I steady-state studies (n = 18/15/39 healthy men) @Jenner2009PramipexoleERpk. The ER 0.75 mg once-daily dose matched the IR 0.25 mg TID dose in steady-state AUC (17.4 vs 16.0 ng·h/mL) with a lower Cmax (0.967 vs 1.09 ng/mL) and a higher Cmin (0.455 vs 0.383 ng/mL): the same total exposure delivered as a flatter concentration–time profile. The profile exchange is a Cmax reduction of ~11% with a Cmin increase of ~19% — the tolerability inference below requires the liability to be peak-driven; for time-above-threshold-driven effects (augmentation, impulse-control effects, somnolence) the higher trough works in the opposite direction, and the PK data do not adjudicate between the two. Steady-state exposure is linear and dose-proportional from 0.375 to 4.5 mg/day, and the food effect is small (AUC fed:fasted upper 90% CI 122.1 at 4.5 mg, within the 80–125% bioequivalence window) @Jenner2009PramipexoleERpk. The formulation difference is therefore kinetic — peak height and timing — not a total-exposure difference.

IR pramipexole is linear at steady state, with a half-life of 12.9 h and renal clearance accounting for approximately 80% of total clearance @Wright1997Pramipexolepk. One consistent PK difference between the formulations: interindividual variability is higher for the ER formulation (AUC coefficient of variation 30.1% vs 21.4% at the higher dose), so a patient who fluctuates between sub- and sup-therapeutic levels on IR may or may not be steadier on ER @Jenner2009PramipexoleERpk.

No RLS or ME/CFS tolerability trial has directly compared the formulations. The plausible inference — that a flatter peak reduces peak-driven side effects (nausea, dizziness, somnolence) — is pharmacokinetically reasonable but unvalidated in either population; a patient who tolerates IR well should not switch formulations on this basis alone, and a patient who does not tolerate IR at the peak may plausibly do better on ER, but that is inference, not evidence.

(Translation gap: healthy male volunteers → ME/CFS patients. The bioequivalence statement itself is a PK property and requires no disease translation; the tolerability inference is unvalidated in RLS and ME/CFS patients.)
(Certainty: 0.70. Evidence type: interventional pharmacokinetic (Phase I, controlled). Population: healthy male volunteers (PK only — not a patient population). Replication: three independent Phase I studies; consistent with the Sifrol/Mirapex PR label. Severity applicability: N/A (PK).)

*Consequence:* For an ME/CFS patient with RLS on pramipexole, the ER formulation is a once-daily option with the same total exposure and a lower, later peak. Choosing it is a kinetics and adherence decision, not a safety decision — no trial shows the ER is better tolerated in RLS or ME/CFS. And the first ER dose does not permit a low starting point: the lowest ER strength (0.375 mg once daily) is ≈0.375 mg/day — three times the IR RLS starting dose, 75% of the 0.5 mg/day ceiling, and exactly at the quantified Takahashi augmentation-risk threshold — so an ER switch in RLS starts near the top of the RLS working band, the doubling titration rhythm is unworkable inside the 0.5 mg/day ceiling, and the higher interindividual variability with ER makes that switch a decision near the top of the usable band, not a convenience one (@pwarn:pramipexole-mecfs-safety).
